feat(deploy): let the trust root's bytes live outside the runtime's storage (#238)

Rung 1 declared these volumes external, so `down -v` can only detach them. That
is the accident that happened and it is closed. It is not the whole of the
accident surface: `docker volume prune` and `docker volume rm` still reach a
named volume, and the second is what an operator reaches for after the first
one refuses.

INNSEGL_TRUST_ROOT_DIR binds each volume to a path under it. The handle is
still removable — nothing can stop that — but removing it now removes a
POINTER. The bytes sit where the runtime does not own them, outside the
checkout, where `git clean` and every volume command are equally powerless.

Measured rather than argued: with it set, a mark was written into the CA
volume, the volume was REMOVED outright — which is stronger than the prune the
rung is named for — and the mark was still on the host path afterwards.

Nothing else in the repository learns about the mode. The volume keeps the
labels the teardown guard reads, so the rung is a deployment choice rather than
a second code path through the stack, and the default is unchanged.

This defends against the accident and nothing else. Anything that can read the
path has the CA key exactly as it did before; what changes what a READER gets
is rung 3, which is the rest of this issue.

